page('01 | Document control','Controlled manual and reading guide','Technical baseline with clearly separated implementation and operating proposals.',
 table(['Document field','Value'],[
 ['Software','NUERS - National Unified Electronic Receipt System'],['Software release / manual edition','1.0.0 / 1.0 | 19 October 2026 | Asia/Manila'],['Document ID','NUERS-TSM-1.0.0-20261019'],['Audience','System owners, implementers, security reviewers, operators, auditors and integration leads'],['Evidence scope','Local application source, supplied requirements and selected official reference material. No live infrastructure audit was performed for this manual.'],['Review status','Prepared technical baseline. Hosting allocation, service commitments and operating plans require management approval.'],['Distribution','Controlled technical reference. Contains no production credentials or customer records.']],[.28,.72]),
 h('How to interpret the evidence labels'),
 p('<b>I - Implemented:</b> behavior visible in the reviewed source. This does not prove that the same release is deployed or that every control has passed live testing. <b>P - Proposed:</b> an operating policy, design or target recommended in this manual. <b>V - Verify:</b> evidence required from deployment, contracts, tests or responsible personnel.'),
 p('The owner confirmed that Dataplugs, Contabo and AWS Singapore/Hong Kong are intended future providers or regions and are not yet in use for the planned arrangement. Their roles are not represented as active infrastructure.'),
 note('Print as A4, actual size, duplex, flip on the long edge. Mirrored margins provide a wider binding edge. Keep this control page with any printed or bound copy; printed copies become uncontrolled when a newer edition is released.'))

page('02 | Scope and architecture','System purpose and boundaries','A merchant transaction platform with separate identity, financial, delivery and regulatory concerns.',
 p('NUERS receives merchant transaction events, records accepted financial data, maintains electronic document views, tracks wallet charges and exposes status to merchant-owned ERP, POS and e-commerce systems. Merchant and administration portals provide controlled access to records, reviews, reports and integration settings.'),
 table(['Boundary','Technical meaning'],[['Source capture','Preserves a copy of an original already issued by the merchant system, including its number, issue date and source identity. It does not allocate a second original invoice number.'],['NUERS issuance','Creates a new original only through the applicable issuance eligibility, authorization and numbering controls. Account activation alone is not issuance authority.'],['NUERS acceptance','A durable local result after validation and the financial database commit. A network acknowledgement and a delivered email are separate states.'],['Government transmission','Internal preparation and controlled delivery interfaces exist. The default provider is blocked; no operational BIR transport or accreditation is asserted here.']],[.26,.74]),
 h('Architecture principles'),
 bullets('One accepted business event produces one financial result; retries return the stored result when the identity and content match.','Live and Sandbox use credential-derived environments. Synthetic tests must not debit real wallets or become Live report entries.','Database commits precede external delivery. Merchant webhook and customer email outcomes cannot roll back an accepted sale.','Every privileged operation is checked at the server boundary; a hidden or disabled browser button is not an authorization control.'),
 note('This manual describes software behavior and proposed operations. It is not a certificate of accreditation, a penetration-test report, a hosting contract or an executed service-level agreement.'))

page('02 | Scope and architecture','Technology and component inventory','Declared dependencies are a build baseline; deployed versions and patch levels require a release inventory.',
 table(['Component','Reviewed baseline and responsibility'],[['Backend','PHP ^8.2 and Laravel ^12.0. HTTP controllers, validation, authorization, transactions, scheduling and application encryption.'],['Web application','React 19.2.4; React Router 7.15.1; TypeScript ~5.9.3; Vite 7.3.1; Tailwind CSS 4.2.1. Merchant and administration interfaces.'],['Database','Relational data accessed through Laravel DB/query services. Production engine version, grants, storage encryption and topology are V items.'],['Queue / cache','Database queue is the configuration default. Shared cache and durable queue must be verified; Redis/SQS configuration support is not proof of use.'],['Document rendering','Shared document view, Node renderer and headless Chromium. Private temporary directory, network restrictions and cleanup support PDF creation.'],['Identity services','Registered-account password login, linked Google OAuth identity, device-bound human API session, TOTP devices and recovery codes.'],['External delivery','Merchant-controlled HTTPS webhook receiver and configured transactional mail provider. Each has independent delivery state.'],['Testing / build','PHPUnit ^11.5.50 declared. Frontend type/build tasks and document renderer build are present; a fresh test result must accompany each release.']],[.25,.75]),
 h('Configuration as an operational asset'),
 p('Maintain a deployment bill of materials with exact lockfile versions, image or package digest, migration set, renderer binary version, environment name and release approver. Record feature flags and public URLs without copying secrets into the inventory. Keep application, worker and renderer releases compatible.'),
 note('Source: composer.json, package.json, config/queue.php, DocumentPdf.php and the build scripts. Version ranges above are declarations, not a claim that every hosting machine runs those exact versions.'))

page('02 | Scope and architecture','System context diagram','D01 - Parties, application boundary, durable records and external recipients.',
 diagram('context','The central application mediates records and delivery. The dashed BIR path is conditional and blocked by the default provider.'),
 p('External systems submit server-to-server requests. Human users enter through a separate session and role boundary. Financial records and private documents remain behind the application. Delivery workers read committed intents and communicate only with the configured recipient systems.'),
 p('Customer mailboxes and merchant callback receivers lie outside NUERS control. A successful callback or mail-server response proves a specific delivery step, not that a person opened the receipt. Official transmission must have its own authentic acknowledgement and evidence.'),
 note('I: logical application paths. P/V: physical placement, external provider activation, network perimeter and official transport.'))

page('03 | End-to-end processing','Transaction acceptance sequence','D02 - The durable commit is the dividing line between intake and external side effects.',
 diagram('sequence','Sequence is simplified to emphasize commit ordering; all authentication and validation failures return before a new financial result is accepted.'),
 p('The connector should save its own outbound event before sending. NUERS checks the credential, environment, business context, request identity, payload and applicable financial rules. A stored identical event returns its original result. A new accepted event commits its records together before success is returned.'),
 p('Email and webhook intents are dispatched asynchronously. If the response is lost after commit, retry the same event identity and unchanged payload or query its status. Never generate a new sale reference merely to overcome a timeout.'),
 note('I: IntegrationTransactionController, MerchantWallet, ApiCustomerReceipt and MerchantWebhooks. A client outbox is a connector responsibility; NUERS cannot make an external ERP commit atomic with its own database.'))

page('03 | End-to-end processing','Data-flow diagram','D03 - Logical processes and stores; this is not a physical network diagram.',
 diagram('dfd','E = external entity; P = process; D = data store. Arrows show authorized logical movement rather than direct database access by clients.'),
 p('P1 resolves authorization and rejects invalid requests. P2 owns acceptance and coordinates inbox, money, document and audit writes. P3 delivers committed notification work. P4 derives scoped reports. P5 keeps government delivery separate from merchant acceptance.'),
 p('The same tenant identifier and environment context must survive every hand-off. Export and status endpoints must enforce these boundaries just as write endpoints do. A report row or receipt URL must never grant access solely because its identifier is known.'),
 note('Database and object-store security remain deployment responsibilities in addition to application checks. See the hosting and access sections for proposed network separation.'))

page('03 | End-to-end processing','Acceptance, money and wallet controls','The fee policy is configurable; collection is tied to a new accepted Live primary sale.',
 p('The reviewed pricing service uses integer centavos. An administrator configures the fee and minimum transaction amount with a reason, revision control and audit evidence. The current default policy is a minimum of PHP 0.00 and a fee of PHP 3.00. A positive minimum exempts amounts strictly below that minimum; equality is chargeable.'),
 table(['Example policy','New finalized Live primary sale','Fee'],[['Minimum PHP 0.00; fee PHP 3.00','PHP 100.00','PHP 3.00'],['Minimum PHP 500.00; fee PHP 3.00','PHP 499.99','PHP 0.00'],['Minimum PHP 500.00; fee PHP 3.00','PHP 500.00','PHP 3.00'],['Same accepted event retried unchanged','Stored result returned','No second debit'],['Supplementary payment copy','References an existing sale','No second sale fee'],['Sandbox event','Synthetic validation and records','No real wallet debit']],[.40,.41,.19]),
 h('Financial acceptance boundary'),
 p('Under the tenant lock, the service checks funds before accepting a chargeable event. An insufficient-wallet response is HTTP 402 and must not create the new financial result or advance its issuance sequence. The wallet movement, transaction, receipt, intake record, readiness preparation and configured notification intents are coordinated by the acceptance workflow.'),
 p('The gross sale amount used for threshold evaluation includes VAT where applicable and is evaluated before withholding deduction. Reporting must sum recorded fees rather than infer all revenue by multiplying row count by the current fee. Policy changes are prospective and do not rewrite earlier ledger entries.'),
 note('P: reconciliation should compare accepted primary sales, recorded fee entries and wallet balances daily. Escalate mismatches; never repair them by editing posted money rows directly.'))

page('03 | End-to-end processing','Idempotency and error recovery','The integration protocol distinguishes a rejected request from an unknown delivery outcome.',
 table(['Outcome','Connector action'],[['201 - accepted','Save NUERS record identifiers and mark the source event successful. Acceptance follows durable recording.'],['200 - identical replay','Use the original result; do not count a new sale or add a second fee.'],['400 / 422 - invalid request','Show the validation reason to an authorized operator; correct the rejected event according to the source-system workflow.'],['401 / 403 - identity or access','Check expiry, revocation, scopes, account eligibility and configured restrictions. Do not expose the secret in logs.'],['402 - insufficient wallet','Show the reason and direct the merchant to fund the wallet. Retry the same event after resolution.'],['404 - status not visible','Check business, branch and environment. A cross-environment lookup must not reveal another record.'],['409 - conflicting identity','Stop automatic replay. Reconcile the original event and content; do not invent a reference to bypass the conflict.'],['429 / transient 5xx / timeout','Use bounded exponential backoff with jitter. Preserve the event and query or retry unchanged after an unknown outcome.']],[.31,.69]),
 p('Stable source identity includes the merchant context, branch, source system and event/document reference. The service also compares canonical content. A copied source document keeps its original number and issue date; changing those fields is not a legitimate duplicate-avoidance technique.'),
 p('The batch intake supports 1-50 complete events in one branch context and is designed for all-or-nothing acceptance. Apply request-size and item limits before transmission. A connector must not interpret the failure of a batch as evidence that a separately submitted event failed.'),
 note('I: schema NUERS-ingestion-1 and current integration contract. Exact field-level examples belong to the merchant connection guide and OpenAPI file; release both with the same application revision.'))

page('03 | End-to-end processing','Live, Sandbox and source eligibility','The environment comes from the API credential, not a caller-controlled request field.',
 table(['Control','Live','Sandbox'],[['Financial records','Accepted business records','Separate encrypted synthetic records'],['Wallet','Real policy and available balance','Virtual simulation; no real debit'],['Numbering','Only authorized issuance path allocates originals','No production sequence mutation'],['Reports','Live business reporting','Separately identified test activity'],['Customer email','Queued when an eligible email is supplied','Suppressed'],['Webhooks','Only verified active Live endpoint','Environment-matched endpoint and test records'],['BIR path','Separate authority and provider gates','No production BIR delivery']],[.28,.36,.36]),
 h('Source capture and automatic setup'),
 p('Verified active merchants can obtain default owned-system connection metadata and generate a secret API credential without a separate approval of that default connection. The merchant portal exposes the assigned source profile and source-system identifier. Existing source identities and historical records remain controlled.'),
 p('Automatic setup is not a blanket approval for historical originals outside the profile scope, another seller, or new government-authorized issuance. A historical-source review remains distinct where required. The server derives the registered establishment context; removing a merchant-facing Branches / Terminals page does not remove backend identity checks.'),
 note('P: use separate ERP outbox namespaces, receiver secrets and monitoring labels per environment. Test migration and reporting queries for environment leakage before each release.'))

page('04 | Data model and lineage','Logical data inventory','Inventory categories support ownership, retention, access review and recovery scoping.',
 table(['Data domain','Typical contents and purpose','Classification'],[['Identity / access','User account, normalized email, role, token hash, MFA device records and recovery-code digests','Restricted'],['Merchant registration','Business identity, TIN, address, owner/representative, review decisions and evidence','Confidential / restricted evidence'],['Integration registry','Credential hash, scope, expiry, environment and source profile','Restricted'],['Financial records','Accepted transaction, line items, taxes, discounts, document identities, wallet debits and deposits','Confidential'],['Private documents','Verification files, filenames, checksums, version history and review notes','Restricted'],['Delivery records','Encrypted customer recipient, webhook endpoint/secret/body, attempts and outcome metadata','Restricted'],['Audit and operations','Actor, event, subject, time, hash-chain linkage, incident and recovery evidence','Confidential / restricted'],['Presentation / analytics','Dedicated receipt logo, settings, scoped reports and derived dashboard totals','Business-controlled; exports confidential']],[.23,.56,.21]),
 p('The logical inventory is not an assertion that every column is encrypted. Selected sensitive fields and private document contents use application encryption. Database-wide, volume and backup encryption require separate infrastructure evidence.'),
 h('Data stewardship'),
 p('Assign a business owner and technical custodian to each domain. Maintain a record of processing purposes, approved readers, retention basis, export destinations and deletion restrictions. Include support systems, backup copies and provider logs in the inventory. Never use unrestricted production data as a test fixture.'),
 note('P: approve a classification policy before broad external distribution of exports. The labels in this manual are a proposed handling baseline.'))

page('04 | Data model and lineage','Data-lineage diagram','D04 - Follow the original event into financial, presentation, delivery and report outputs.',
 diagram('lineage','Financial provenance and visual branding are separate branches. Current document copies may use current saved branding while retaining original seller, items and financial values.'),
 p('A source event is normalized and bound to a stable identity and content hash. The accepted inbox result connects external references to NUERS transaction and receipt identifiers. Wallet entries and audit events allow a reviewer to trace the acceptance without recomputing history from today\'s settings.'),
 p('Reports are derived views, not new financial originals. A webhook carries limited status metadata rather than customer details or receipt imagery. A customer PDF is a rendered copy whose email-delivery status is tracked independently.'),
 note('I: ElectronicDocumentView applies current saved visual branding to document copies. P: when exact rendered-copy reproducibility is required, retain an authorized PDF artifact, its hash, render timestamp and branding revision.'))

page('04 | Data model and lineage','Lineage keys and reconciliation rules','Identifiers must stay linked across operations, investigation and customer support.',
 table(['Origin / key','Transformation or linkage','Verification'],[['source_event_id + external_reference','Stable external event/document identity within the scoped source','Unchanged retry maps to original accepted IDs'],['source_profile_id + source_system','Seller and owned-system context; scope and effective period checks','Reject conflicting seller, scope or unsupported historical period'],['Canonical payload hash','Normalized input compared for duplicate/conflict decisions','Same identity + changed content is a conflict'],['NUERS transaction / receipt IDs','Intake result links to financial snapshot and document view','Status, report and document resolve to the same tenant'],['Wallet movement','Fee charged under the policy used at acceptance','Opening + credits - debits reconciles to closing balance'],['Audit event / chain head','Actor, action, subject, timestamp and previous hash','Chain recomputation and independent checkpoint match'],['Delivery ID / event ID','Email or webhook attempt references committed subject','Retries do not create a new financial event'],['Export filter context','Environment, business, dates and sales category bound to output','Displayed/exported totals reconcile to the same selection']],[.31,.39,.30]),
 h('Time and financial precision'),
 p('Carry an explicit offset on external event timestamps. Preserve the original business occurrence time separately from ingestion, commit, review and delivery times. Use UTC for event ordering where implemented and label Philippines business views as Asia/Manila. Reconcile using integer centavos and documented rounding, not floating-point approximations.'),
 note('P: a reconciliation evidence pack should record the query period, timezone, environment, record counts, monetary totals, exceptions, reviewer and export hash. Do not place full buyer identifiers in a general incident ticket.'))

page('04 | Data model and lineage','Electronic documents and receipt emails','The receipt uses its own branding configuration, independent of the merchant profile image.',
 p('The profile image identifies the business in portal and registry views. Invoice Settings maintains a dedicated receipt logo plus template, colors, font, footer and related presentation options. The shared document view preserves recorded seller identity, items and totals while applying current permitted visual branding to copies.'),
 h('Customer email flow'),
 bullets('The accepting Live API workflow reads the supplied customer email and creates a document-email delivery record and notification intent inside the accepted-sale transaction.','The recipient is encrypted. The asynchronous worker renders the shared e-receipt PDF and sends through the configured mail transport. SMTP is not performed in the intake request.','Sandbox returns a suppressed email state. Missing customer email returns not_requested. An accepted sale remains accepted if later mail preparation or delivery fails.'),
 table(['Delivery state','Meaning and response'],[['queued / preparing / sending','Work is in progress; do not promise inbox delivery.'],['smtp_accepted','The mail server accepted the message. This is not proof of customer receipt, reading or absence of a later bounce.'],['uncertain','Outcome cannot be confirmed; investigate before resending to avoid duplicates.'],['failed / expired / cancelled','Use the recorded reason and an authorized resend/copy workflow after the cause is resolved.']],[.35,.65]),
 p('The PDF service uses a private temporary directory and an isolated Chromium profile. It passes only an allowlisted process environment, renders vetted content with network access restricted and removes temporary files in cleanup. Runtime availability and sandbox operation must be verified on the actual host.'),
 note('P: establish the mail provider, sender-domain authentication, bounce handling, retention and data-location register before external customer delivery is relied on operationally.'))

page('05 | Transmission and callbacks','Merchant webhook protocol','Signed notifications provide asynchronous status; the receiver must still deduplicate.',
 p('The owner configures a public HTTPS callback for the appropriate environment. Endpoint verification uses a challenge; a successful verification leaves it paused until activation. Configured events include transaction.accepted, source.received, invoice.issued and payment.recorded. Secrets are separate from API credentials and versioned.'),
 table(['Protocol / safeguard','Reviewed behavior'],[['Signature','HMAC-SHA256 over timestamp + "." + event_id + "." + raw_body; X-NUERS-Signature contains the v1= prefix.'],['Headers','X-NUERS-Event, X-NUERS-Timestamp, X-NUERS-Environment and X-NUERS-Key-Version accompany the JSON event.'],['Destination','Public DNS hostname, HTTPS port 443, no credentials/query/fragment, no NUERS self-callback. All resolved addresses checked against private/reserved ranges.'],['Transport','DNS resolved again and pinned; certificate/hostname validation on; no proxy or redirect fallback; 3-second connect and 8-second total timeout; response limited to 16 KiB.'],['Durability','Encrypted body and SHA-256 content hash; leased attempts. Retry budget is 12 attempts within the ordinary one-day deadline, with exponential delay and jitter.'],['Stop conditions','Redirects and selected permanent HTTP rejections dead-letter; changed/revoked endpoint or account can cancel pending work.']],[.25,.75]),
 p('The receiver should verify the signature against the exact raw request body, enforce an agreed timestamp tolerance, compare signatures in constant time and persist the event ID before acknowledging. Return 2xx after durable receipt, then process internally. Duplicate callback delivery must be harmless.'),
 note('I: current merchant webhook services. P/V: the merchant receiver and a real end-to-end delivery test. Callback payloads intentionally omit customer details, monetary amounts, secrets and document images.'))

page('05 | Transmission and callbacks','Government transmission boundary','NUERS receipt, readiness and official acceptance must remain distinguishable.',
 p('The current merchant API exposes NUERS success or an error reason. Its accepted response strips bir_status and related internal BIR preparation fields. This simplifies the connector contract without converting a local success into a government acknowledgement.'),
 table(['Stage','Evidence and operational treatment'],[['Local acceptance','Stored financial result and NUERS identifiers. Safe to report as recorded by NUERS after success.'],['Internal preparation','Readiness model and source/authority checks; this is not a verified government wire message.'],['Delivery eligibility','Current taxpayer, branch, environment, authorization, source coverage and solution controls must pass.'],['Official transport','The reviewed default BlockedProvider returns not ready and refuses send/inquiry. A verified adapter and credentials are required before enabling a real path.'],['Official acknowledgement','Only an authentic provider response/inquiry can establish the relevant external outcome. Never fabricate acknowledgements to clear a dashboard.'],['Unknown outcome','Reconcile by authentic inquiry before replay. Preserve the job, evidence and prior attempt history.']],[.29,.71]),
 h('Operational release gate'),
 p('A future transport release must identify the authoritative interface specification, mapping tests, signing/certificate requirements, authorized endpoint, taxpayer scope and reporting obligations. Review duplicates where source originals may already have been reported through another system. Keep credentials out of screenshots and source control.'),
 p('The delivery and reconciliation commands expose internal work and incident reminders. Their existence does not prove a running scheduler or a successful external transmission. Operators must inspect recorded outcomes and preserve the provider evidence.'),
 note('V: accreditation, authority, live transport, service credentials and provider acknowledgements. This manual makes no representation that these have been granted or independently verified.'))

page('06 | Hosting and data location','Planned hosting disclosure','Owner-confirmed planning status: the named providers and regions are not yet in use for this planned arrangement.',
 table(['Provider / region','Disclosure and decision still required'],[['Dataplugs','Planned candidate. Official material lists Hong Kong facilities. Exact contracted facility, product, tenancy, role, support access and data placement must be selected and recorded. [R2]'],['Contabo','Planned candidate. Public provider material describes Singapore availability; exact order location, current service availability and recovery capability require procurement confirmation. [R3]'],['AWS Singapore','Planned region ap-southeast-1. Decide whether used for compute, backup, logs or recovery; none is asserted as deployed. [R1]'],['AWS Hong Kong','Planned region ap-east-1. Region enablement and each required service must be checked before provisioning. No cross-region replication is asserted. [R1]'],['Current production location','Not independently established by this manual. Prior UI addresses or a provider hostname are not sufficient evidence of the current data residency or topology.']],[.28,.72]),
 h('Decision principles'),
 p('Choose one authoritative primary database writer and a documented recovery site. Do not distribute active writers across providers without an explicitly engineered consistency and failover design. A lower-cost compute provider can be combined with independently managed backups, but the team must own network, database, key and restore operations.'),
 p('Complete procurement, privacy review, region restrictions, provider access terms, monitoring, backup restoration and failover drills before declaring the proposed design operational. Provider certifications or facility features do not automatically certify the NUERS workload.'),
 note('P: the following design is a decision framework, not an approved allocation. No migration, provider purchase or production configuration change was performed to prepare this PDF.'))

page('06 | Hosting and data location','Target hosting architecture','D05 - Proposed roles and candidate locations; all dashed paths require implementation and evidence.',
 diagram('hosting','Provider candidate boxes do not indicate that all four locations host production copies. Assign each actual role in an approved deployment register before enabling data movement.'),
 p('The primary site contains the authoritative database, private objects and application services. A separate recovery site receives approved encrypted backups or replication. Independent backup custody reduces the risk that a compromised primary administrator can destroy every recoverable copy.'),
 p('Cross-provider transport needs authenticated encryption and restricted routes. Recovery promotion must fence the former writer and pause side-effect workers. Singapore and Hong Kong placement must be deliberate for databases, backups, logs, keys and support access, rather than inferred from the application URL.'),
 note('P: no active-active database, automatic regional failover, cloud KMS integration or immutable offsite copy is claimed as implemented by this diagram.'))

page('06 | Hosting and data location','Data-residency and subprocessor register','Record the location of every copy, not only the application server.',
 table(['Asset / processor','Location decision and required evidence'],[['Production database','Primary provider, facility/region, account/project ID, replica locations, engine, encryption and administrative access. Status: V.'],['Verification documents / receipt assets','Storage backend, object region, temporary render location, replication and public-access restrictions. Status: V.'],['Backups / transaction logs','Source and destination regions, schedule, retention, object lock, key location, restore record and deletion method. Status: P/V.'],['Application / security logs','Collector and archive regions, redaction, retention, provider access and query permissions. Status: P/V.'],['Secrets / recovery keys','Custody platform, region, administrators, break-glass mechanism and key recovery evidence. Status: P/V.'],['Transactional email','Mail provider, processing/storage locations, delivery logs, subprocessors, retention and sender authentication. Status: V.'],['Google identity','Identity provider and data exchanged; document applicable contractual and privacy disclosures. Tokens and identifiers require controlled handling.'],['Merchant webhook receiver','Merchant-selected public endpoint and environment. The merchant controls the destination; NUERS must enforce endpoint and payload restrictions.'],['Support / monitoring tools','Ticket attachments, traces, analytics, remote access and any copied personal data must be included. Status: V.']],[.30,.70]),
 p('The accountable owner should sign the register before activation and after every provider, region or subprocessor change. Attach a data-flow record, contract/DPA reference, security review, deletion/exit procedure and access owner. Do not include credentials in this register.'),
 note('P: cross-border processing requires a documented privacy and contractual review. No conclusion of legal compliance is inferred merely from selecting Singapore or Hong Kong.'))

page('06 | Hosting and data location','Network zones and deployment controls','Proposed infrastructure baseline to support the application controls.',
 table(['Zone','Allowed flows and controls'],[['Public edge','HTTPS client traffic to approved web/API routes. Configure certificates, request limits, rate controls and WAF rules; restrict direct origin bypass.'],['Application','Only edge-to-app traffic on documented service ports. Dedicated service identity; no cloud/root administration from the runtime.'],['Data','Database and cache reachable only by authorized services and controlled maintenance access. No public database listener.'],['Private objects','Tenant-authorized application reads; no anonymous verification-document URLs. Limit backup writers and readers separately.'],['Workers / egress','Only required mail, approved callback and future authorized provider traffic. Monitor destination changes and failed egress.'],['Administration','Named accounts, MFA, controlled access gateway, time-bounded privilege and audit trail. Avoid shared Plesk/root identities.'],['Recovery','Isolated restore validation with outgoing mail, callbacks and government delivery disabled until reconciliation and cutover approval.']],[.25,.75]),
 p('Before multiple application nodes are introduced, verify shared storage behavior, distributed locks, atomic quotas, scheduler exclusion and worker leases. A filesystem that is local to one machine is not automatically available to another. Choose a tested object/storage adapter and migration approach before moving private files.'),
 p('TLS 1.3 should be preferred and TLS 1.2 retained only where necessary; disable legacy protocols and weak cipher configuration. Verify every TLS hop, including database and provider connections, at deployment. Application HSTS and certificate checks do not establish the edge cipher configuration. [R6]'),
 note('P/V: infrastructure hardening, origin access, firewall rules, TLS scans, shared-service topology and administrator access evidence.'))

page('07 | Security and identity','Security control baseline','Application evidence is distinct from infrastructure assurance and independently tested effectiveness.',
 table(['Control area','Reviewed implementation','Evidence still required'],[['Identity','Registered user checks, linked Google identity, token hashes and device binding','Production configuration; account/role review'],['MFA','TOTP devices, replay counters and single-use recovery codes','Enrollment coverage; recovery drill'],['Tenant access','Server-side role, merchant and team scope checks','Negative cross-tenant tests for every route/export'],['Application encryption','Laravel Crypt on selected sensitive fields and private file frames','Key custody; host/DB/backup encryption'],['API integrity','Strict schema, canonical identities, durable inbox and duplicate handling','Concurrency, replay and failure-injection tests'],['Outbound webhooks','Signed events, destination validation, DNS pinning and bounded transport','Real merchant receiver verification and test'],['Browser hardening','CSP, frame denial, nosniff, no-referrer and private API cache headers','Observed deployed headers and TLS behavior'],['Audit','Hash chain and separately keyed checkpoint command','Independent custody and restore verification'],['Recovery','Evidence-oriented recovery dashboard and workflow','Actual backups, independent restores and measured recovery'],['Threat detection','Application records and operational reminders','Central alert routing, on-call response and tested detections']],[.22,.40,.38]),
 p('Run security tests against both successful and rejected operations. Test object-level authorization, identifier guessing, role downgrade, revoked keys, stale sessions, altered payloads, duplicate events and unauthorized export requests. Review custom browser token handling and device storage against the XSS threat model.'),
 note('P: use a documented risk register with owners, severity, compensating controls and expiry dates. The presence of a control in code is not a substitute for independent security testing.'))

page('07 | Security and identity','Identity and authorization paths','D06 - Human authentication and machine integration use different credential lifecycles.',
 diagram('identity','A verified login permits only actions allowed by role, ownership, team grant and business state. Machine credentials do not inherit an administrator session.'),
 p('Human access is resolved to a registered user and role, then scoped to the target business and action. MFA assurance attaches to the current login. Machine requests use a separate hashed API credential with environment, scope, expiry and applicable network restrictions.'),
 p('Account activation, document review, integration metadata creation and permission to issue a new original are distinct decisions. The interface should explain an unmet condition, while the backend enforces it. Changing presentation or navigation must not remove a security boundary.'),
 note('I: ApiAccess, ApiBoundary, MerchantTeamAccess and merchant key/context services. P/V: an approved role-permission catalogue and periodic access certification.'))

page('07 | Security and identity','Access model and segregation of duties','Recommended role governance layered on the implemented role and tenant checks.',
 table(['Actor','Permitted responsibility','Separation requirement'],[['Merchant owner','Business-owned settings, key generation, authorized team grants and wallet visibility','Cannot grant government authority or view another merchant'],['Merchant team member','Only assigned business functions and scope','No implicit owner credential-management rights'],['NUERS reviewer','Review documents and proposed identity corrections within assigned authority','Retain evidence, reason and actor; avoid reviewing own changes'],['Finance operator','Reconcile deposits, wallet movements and fee policy under authorized workflow','Separate approval of adjustments from preparation'],['Security administrator','Account access, incident containment and security configuration','No unreviewed rewriting of financial history'],['Platform operator','Deployments, monitoring, backup and recovery operations','No routine unrestricted customer-data access'],['Auditor / reviewer','Read evidence and scoped exports as authorized','Read-only by default; no operational credential reuse'],['Service identity','A bounded application, queue or integration task','No interactive human login; least privilege and rotation']],[.24,.42,.34]),
 h('Joiner, mover and leaver procedure - proposed'),
 p('Register the person, accountable manager and approved role. Confirm identity, enroll MFA where required, grant the minimum scope and record approval. When duties change, remove old grants before adding new ones. On departure or suspected compromise, revoke sessions and keys, remove team grants, reassign operational ownership and verify that access is denied.'),
 p('Review privileged access monthly and all business/service access quarterly as an initial policy. Use a controlled emergency account with named custody, explicit incident linkage and post-use review. Fill the roster with actual people before the service framework is adopted.'),
 note('P: two-person approval and review frequencies are governance recommendations unless a particular server workflow already enforces them.'))

page('07 | Security and identity','Login, sessions and multiple MFA devices','Once-per-login assurance remains subject to expiry, revocation and permission checks.',
 p('Google sign-in is restricted to a verified email that matches a unique existing registered account. A linked stable Google subject must also match. New identity linking is limited by the implemented Gmail or verified Workspace checks. OAuth state and a short-lived single-use handoff protect the exchange; successful OAuth is followed by NUERS MFA where required.'),
 table(['Mechanism','Reviewed behavior'],[['Human API token','SHA-256 lookup, expiration and absolute-session checks. X-NUERS-Device is compared to the stored device digest. Missing device binding is denied outside tests.'],['Session assurance','The current verified login carries MFA assurance; routine actions do not trigger a repeated 15-minute challenge. A new login, expiry or revocation requires the applicable checks again.'],['Authenticator','Six-digit TOTP; 160-bit random secret; 30-second step; current/adjacent step window; accepted counter must be newer than the prior counter.'],['Multiple devices','Up to five independent TOTP devices; each has its own encrypted secret and replay state. Adding/removing requires the implemented password and existing-factor/recovery controls.'],['Setup / recovery','Pending setup expires after ten minutes. Recovery codes are account-level, hashed and single-use. The final active authenticator cannot be removed through the normal device-removal flow.']],[.26,.74]),
 p('Session timeout values are configurable. The reviewed human API policy defaults differ from Laravel cookie-session defaults; record both effective configurations rather than treating them as one timeout. Multiple authenticators are not permission to share a login or a guarantee of concurrent independent browser sessions.'),
 note('P: require recovery identity checks, owner notification and revocation review. Never ask a user to send an authenticator seed, current code or recovery code in a support ticket.'))

page('07 | Security and identity','API credential lifecycle','Issue a secret for a specific integration and environment; show it once and store only its digest.',
 p('The merchant creation flow collects the integration name and environment. The server assigns the registered business context and checks the verified owner login and active account. The generated secret uses a NUERS environment prefix and random material; the application stores a SHA-256 digest for authentication rather than a recoverable API secret.'),
 table(['Lifecycle step','Required practice'],[['Create','Use a descriptive system name. Keep Live and Sandbox credentials separate. Confirm business/source context and allowed actions.'],['Store','Copy the one-time secret to the merchant backend secret store. Never embed it in browser JavaScript, mobile bundles, repositories, URLs or receipts.'],['Constrain','Apply available permission and exact-IP restrictions. Defaults include transaction read/write, 90-day expiry and daily limits of 50,000 for Live or 5,000 for Sandbox. Limits are not capacity guarantees.'],['Use','Send over HTTPS in the documented header or bearer form. Conflicting authentication forms are rejected. Preserve response IDs and sanitized errors.'],['Rotate','Create a replacement, update the controlled connector, confirm status/acceptance using the right environment, then revoke the old key. Track both owners and expiry dates.'],['Revoke','Immediately disable suspected or retired credentials. Review recent events and callback destinations; do not delete financial evidence.']],[.22,.78]),
 p('The current merchant connection is a secret-key protocol. This manual does not invent a merchant public/private signing-key pair or imply that API keys are government signing certificates. Webhook signing secrets and future provider-issued credentials have separate purposes and custody.'),
 note('P: alert before expiry, inventory keys by owner and integration, and test revocation propagation. Do not run a Live financial test solely to confirm that a credential was issued.'))

page('08 | Encryption and key management','Encryption and integrity matrix','Encryption, hashing and signatures solve different problems and need different recovery procedures.',
 table(['Asset / channel','Current mechanism','Limit / operational requirement'],[['Selected sensitive fields','Laravel Crypt; configured AES-256-CBC with framework authentication/MAC','Protect APP_KEY; not every database column is encrypted'],['Private verification files','Authenticated encrypted frames of up to 1 MiB; length and SHA-256 checked before exposure','Protect private storage and temporary plaintext space; retain matching metadata'],['Passwords','Framework password hashing through Hash facilities','Record effective algorithm/work factor; test upgrades without weakening existing hashes'],['Human / merchant tokens','SHA-256 credential digest comparison','High-entropy secrets; hash is not a backup of the original secret'],['MFA seeds','Reversible application encryption for TOTP verification','Restrict secret access and erase expired setup material'],['Recovery codes','Hashed, single-use account recovery values','Original codes must be retained privately by the user'],['Webhook event','HMAC-SHA256 with a versioned callback secret; encrypted stored body','Receiver verifies raw bytes, event ID and timestamp; no public-key certificate claim'],['Audit checkpoint','Independent HMAC-SHA256 key over checkpoint evidence','Separate custody and offsite verification required'],['Network / disks / backups','HTTPS use/checks in application; host-wide encryption not established','V: TLS termination, database transport, disks, backups and key-service settings']],[.24,.40,.36]),
 p('SHA-256 detects a content mismatch or permits comparison of a high-entropy token digest; it does not conceal ordinary personal information. HMAC authenticity depends on key secrecy. Application encryption cannot protect plaintext while it is legitimately processed by the running application or prevent misuse by an overly privileged operator.'),
 note('P: maintain a cryptographic inventory with algorithm, purpose, key owner, location, creation/rotation dates, dependencies and recovery test evidence.'))

page('08 | Encryption and key management','Key hierarchy and separation','D07 - Current application keys and proposed managed-key evolution.',
 diagram('keys','The lower row is a proposed future design. The current application uses Laravel encryption; no deployed cloud KMS envelope-encryption integration is asserted.'),
 p('APP_KEY enables decryption of application-protected data. The audit checkpoint key should have separate custody so that a database/application compromise does not automatically control independent checkpoint verification. API secrets are verified by digest, while webhook secrets must be recoverable for signing.'),
 p('A future vault or KMS design must define service identities, key policies, region placement, audit access, emergency recovery and migration compatibility. Moving a key into a cloud console without updating the application and restoration process does not establish a managed-key architecture.'),
 note('P/V: deployment secret storage, separate administrators, automated secret delivery and tested recovery of each encryption dependency. Never print keys in this manual or in a provider inventory.'))

page('08 | Encryption and key management','Key rotation and recovery runbook','Preserve decryptability while reducing exposure; never replace APP_KEY casually.',
 table(['Phase','Procedure and evidence'],[['1. Inventory','Identify data protected by the key: MFA seeds, private files, sensitive payloads, endpoint secrets and mail recipients. Record current version references without exposing values.'],['2. Prepare','Verify an encrypted backup and independent recovery path. Restrict access to key material. Test rotation against a representative isolated copy and all dependent services.'],['3. Introduce','Use a reviewed versioned-key rollout. Laravel supports previous application keys, but effective configuration and every data format must be tested. Keep old decryption available during transition.'],['4. Re-encrypt','Migrate data in bounded, restartable batches with audit evidence. Verify a sample and aggregate counts. Avoid reading secrets into general application logs.'],['5. Validate','Test document retrieval, MFA, callback signing, email rendering and restore operations. Confirm new writes use the intended key version.'],['6. Retire','Retire an old key only after all required retained data and backups have a viable decryption path or approved expiry. Record approver, date and destruction evidence.']],[.23,.77]),
 h('Compromise response'),
 p('If an application key may be exposed, treat confidentiality of all data protected by it as potentially affected. Isolate the cause, preserve evidence, revoke compromised access and determine which data was accessible. Coordinate credential rotation and privacy review. Rotation alone does not undo prior disclosure.'),
 p('Maintain a sealed or independently controlled recovery method with at least two authorized custodians under the proposed policy. Test recovery without publishing the key or restoring outbound delivery into an uncontrolled environment. Loss of a required decryption key can make a technically intact backup unusable.'),
 note('P: this is a proposed operating procedure, not an automated rotation feature or evidence that a key ceremony has already occurred.'))

page('09 | Audit, privacy and secure change','Audit integrity and evidence custody','A hash chain is tamper-evident evidence, not independent immutable storage by itself.',
 p('BirCompliance appends audit events under a locked chain head. The event hash includes the previous hash, event name, subject, actor, serialized details and UTC timestamp. Integrity verification recomputes the chain and compares the final head. This helps detect changes relative to a trusted reference.'),
 p('The checkpoint command verifies the chain and signs a checkpoint using a separately configured HMAC key. Its local file is not automatically an offsite immutable record. An attacker with sufficient database and application-key privileges could otherwise replace both records and local evidence.'),
 table(['Evidence type','Handling baseline - proposed'],[['Audit events','Restrict write permissions to controlled services; prohibit ordinary edit/delete access; review gaps and failures.'],['Independent checkpoints','Export to a separate account/custodian, verify signatures and retention, and test retrieval during recovery.'],['Security logs','Record event category, actor reference, environment, outcome, request correlation and time; redact tokens, passwords, buyer details and callback secrets.'],['Forensic copies','Capture source, acquisition time, collector, SHA-256, access history and custody transfers. Preserve originals read-only.'],['Administrative exports','Limit recipients, encrypt transport and storage, retain a reason and expiry, and revoke temporary access after review.']],[.29,.71]),
 h('Operational review'),
 p('Monitor audit-write failures, chain mismatch, clock drift, unexpected privileged actions and missing worker heartbeats. A dashboard showing records is not proof of complete logging. Store evidence of daily checks and investigate when collection stops.'),
 note('I: chain and checkpoint generation. P/V: independent custody, immutable retention, privileged database restrictions and a measured detection process.'))

page('09 | Audit, privacy and secure change','Private uploads and document review','Large evidence files require controlled staging, integrity checks and bounded processing.',
 p('The verification service accepts PDF, PNG and JPG up to 100 x 1024 x 1024 bytes, displayed as 100 MB. Upload sessions support bounded chunks; the general API request-size cap is separate from the total document limit. Proxy and PHP limits must align with the chosen upload path.'),
 bullets('Validate the document type, actual content and size. Stage files outside the public web root and bind upload sessions to the authorized registration/account context.','Store content in authenticated encrypted frames; preserve plaintext length and SHA-256 metadata. Before reviewer exposure, decrypt and verify every byte against the expected size and digest.','Version replacements independently. A newly uploaded current version requires a fresh review; previous copies and decisions remain in history.','Authorize previews and downloads by owner or permitted reviewer. Record access where implemented and avoid public reusable document links.','Remove expired unclaimed uploads through the scheduled cleanup; retain claimed evidence under the approved retention schedule.'),
 h('Further hardening before production reliance - proposed'),
 p('Add or verify malware scanning, quarantine decisions, resource limits and suspicious-content handling. File-type validation alone is not malware detection. Review PDF/image parser updates and isolate costly rendering. The current private-stream verifier may use temporary plaintext storage; verify host encryption, permissions, cleanup and capacity for that path.'),
 p('Keep reviewers focused on the original evidence and recorded decision. Branding changes do not prove identity. Identity and tax-profile corrections follow review workflows; a direct profile-image update does not confer account verification.'),
 note('I: 100 MiB application limit, private frame storage and integrity checks. V: reverse-proxy limits, malware controls and operational review coverage.'))

page('09 | Audit, privacy and secure change','Privacy, retention and controlled deletion','Retention must follow the applicable purpose, record class and approved legal basis.',
 table(['Record class','Policy decision required'],[['Financial originals and adjustments','Approve retention period, fiscal/legal obligations, legal holds and correction rules. Preserve provenance; do not silently rewrite posted financial history.'],['Registration and identity evidence','Define access, necessity, review retention, replacement history and secure disposal after permitted expiry.'],['Customer receipt data','Limit to receipt processing and authorized support; document the merchant relationship, notice, email provider and delivery retention.'],['Security and audit records','Set investigative retention and access controls. Retain incident/legal-hold material beyond ordinary expiry where authorized.'],['Sandbox and development data','Use synthetic or de-identified fixtures. Set automatic expiry where implemented and avoid importing production identity documents.'],['Backups and replicas','Specify lifecycle, legal holds, deletion lag and how deletion/restriction requests are honored after a restore.'],['Temporary files and exports','Minimize lifetime, use restricted storage and document download/export recipient responsibility.']],[.30,.70]),
 p('Proposed deletion workflow: verify requester authority and scope, check holds and regulatory requirements, identify dependent records and copies, obtain the required approval, preserve authorized audit evidence, execute a scoped deletion and verify the result. Reconcile restored backups against the deletion register before re-opening access.'),
 p('The owner\'s earlier test-account reset requests are not a general authorization to remove other merchants or statutory records. The manual does not prescribe one universal retention period; the DPO/legal owner and records owner must approve the schedule for the actual service and jurisdiction.'),
 note('P/V: privacy impact assessment, processing agreements, subprocessor register, retention schedule and data-subject request procedure.'))

page('09 | Audit, privacy and secure change','Secure development and release procedure','Every production change needs a reproducible artifact and a bounded rollback plan.',
 table(['Gate','Required evidence - proposed unless stated otherwise'],[['Change definition','Problem, intended behavior, affected tenant/environment, data migration and compatibility impact.'],['Review','Peer review of access boundaries, financial logic, cryptography use and sensitive output. Separate high-risk approval from implementation where feasible.'],['Automated checks','Backend tests, frontend type/build checks, document-renderer tests, negative authorization and environment isolation. Record commands, revision and results.'],['Security validation','Dependency/SBOM review, secret scan, upload/SSRF controls, rate behavior and threat-specific tests. Do not treat a passing build as a security assessment.'],['Release package','Immutable build digest, dependency locks, migrations, rollback scripts, operator notes and documentation revision.'],['Deployment','Backup readiness, maintenance/traffic plan, reviewed configuration, migration execution and worker restart compatibility.'],['Verification','Read-only smoke checks, authenticated role checks, queue health, document rendering and environment separation; no unapproved Live sale.'],['Close / rollback','Record measured outcome, compare to acceptance criteria and roll back on defined triggers. Forward-fix irreversible schema/data changes only through a reviewed plan.']],[.26,.74]),
 p('The security:deployment-check command evaluates basic configuration conditions such as HTTPS URL, debug setting, shared cache, durable queue, MFA, checkpoint key and reviewed-release reference. It explicitly cannot prove WAF effectiveness, least-privilege grants, key custody, high availability, correct restores, regulatory authority or measured capacity.'),
 note('I: a configuration-check command exists. P/V: release governance, automated pipeline enforcement and completed independent tests.'))

page('10 | Operations and continuity','Operational routines and worker inventory','Scheduling declarations are not evidence that the server scheduler is running.',
 table(['Work item','Declared cadence / behavior','Operator check'],[['Internal EIS preparation','Every minute; creates missing internal records without network delivery','Backlog, conflicts and worker errors'],['EIS reconciliation','Every five minutes; internal reminders for exceptions and overdue cases','Uncertain/dead-letter work and owner assignment'],['Transactional notifications','Every minute, bounded work; dispatch flag controls external sending','Queue age, uncertain outcomes, sender health'],['Notification reconciliation','Hourly','Deduplication and reminder freshness'],['Merchant webhooks','Every minute, bounded runtime and overlap guard','Heartbeat, oldest pending event, delivery failures'],['MFA setup cleanup','Every ten minutes','Expired pending secrets erased'],['Unclaimed upload cleanup','Daily','Expired staging removed; claimed evidence preserved'],['Audit checkpoint','Explicit command; offsite export needs an operational process','Chain validity, signature and independent custody']],[.30,.36,.34]),
 h('Daily operator checklist - proposed'),
 p('Review application availability, queue age, failed jobs, database capacity, wallet reconciliation, audit integrity, backup completion, restore-point freshness and security alerts. Confirm that alerts reach a staffed channel. Document exceptions with an owner and due time; do not clear red indicators merely by changing a status field.'),
 p('Before maintenance, identify work in flight, pause only affected dispatchers, capture queue counts and preserve idempotency state. After maintenance, verify new commits and resume workers gradually. External email and webhook outcomes require their own evidence even if the application is healthy.'),
 note('I: routes/console.php defines these schedules. V: cron/service-manager configuration, effective time zone, worker supervision and real heartbeat evidence.'))

page('10 | Operations and continuity','Monitoring and online/offline semantics','An indicator must name the dependency it actually measures.',
 table(['Signal','Truthful meaning','Must not imply'],[['Browser online','The browser reports a network interface/connectivity state','NUERS API or provider is reachable'],['NUERS responding','A defined application health/status request received a valid response','Database writes, receipts or external transport all work'],['Integrated','Accepted external Live intake with current eligible credentials','Real-time connectivity or BIR connection'],['Authenticated API ready','Current session/key and scoped status checks pass','Unlimited funds or issuance authority'],['Worker healthy','Heartbeat and bounded queue processing evidence are current','Every callback recipient is available'],['BIR readiness / outcome','Separate recorded eligibility/provider evidence','Success solely because NUERS accepted the sale'],['Backup / recovery ready','Current verified backup and restore evidence against policy','Readiness from a green server icon alone']],[.28,.40,.32]),
 h('Proposed operational dashboard'),
 p('Track eligible request success, server errors, p50/p95/p99 latency, database lock time, queue age, worker heartbeat, failed/uncertain notifications, webhook dead letters, wallet discrepancies, storage capacity, certificate expiry and audit-chain failures. Break down metrics by environment and service, with tenant-level views limited to authorized staff.'),
 p('The offline connector should retain its own durable outbox and show pending or unknown state clearly. Never display a sale as successfully recorded solely because it entered a local queue. Browser retry storage is not the authoritative ERP backup. The same principle applies when a page is cached or a previously healthy indicator is stale.'),
 note('P: display last-checked time and a stale state. Test DNS failure, offline mode, expired session, API 5xx and an unavailable callback independently.'))

page('10 | Operations and continuity','Backup and recovery policy','Proposed baseline; actual recovery points and times must be measured in drills.',
 table(['Asset','Proposed protection','Verification'],[['Relational database','Scheduled encrypted full backups plus transaction-log / point-in-time recovery where supported','Restore to an isolated host; reconcile committed transaction and wallet totals'],['Private documents','Versioned encrypted copies coordinated with metadata and checksums','Restore and decrypt representative files; compare length and SHA-256'],['Application/configuration','Release artifacts, schema version and sanitized configuration inventory','Rebuild service and renderer from documented versions'],['Keys / secrets','Separate secure recovery custody with restricted access','Demonstrate decryption and signing without exposing key material'],['Audit evidence','Chain plus independently held signed checkpoints','Recompute restored chain and compare external checkpoint'],['Work queues','Durable inbox/outbox and attempt records in consistent backups','Reconcile uncertain side effects before resuming dispatch']],[.25,.43,.32]),
 p('Initial planning objectives are an RPO of at most 15 minutes and an RTO of at most four hours for the core service. These are proposed engineering targets, not achieved results or contractual commitments. Confirm cost, replication/log support, data volume, key recovery and staffing before approval.'),
 p('Use independent credentials and a separate administrative domain for offsite copies. AWS S3 Object Lock is a candidate for retention-protected object versions; mode, retention, lifecycle and recovery access require deliberate configuration and testing. Its availability does not mean it is enabled for NUERS. [R5]'),
 p('Set daily backup verification, monthly sample restoration and quarterly full recovery exercises as an initial proposed schedule. Re-run after material schema, storage or encryption changes. The BCDR dashboard should record evidence and measured outcomes rather than manufacture successful backup jobs.'),
 note('P/V: backup schedule, immutable offsite custody, recovery objectives, runbooks and drill results. No completed recovery exercise is claimed in this edition.'))

page('10 | Operations and continuity','Disaster-recovery sequence','D08 - A controlled single-writer recovery prevents split-brain and duplicate side effects.',
 diagram('recovery','Proposed sequence. The incident commander and service owner authorize cutover only after data, keys, identities and uncertain outcomes have been reconciled.'),
 p('Recover database, private objects, application versions, configuration and keys as a compatible set. Confirm financial totals, audit integrity, source identities, document checksums, sequence positions and pending delivery records. Keep outgoing mail, webhooks and future BIR transport paused during isolated restore tests.'),
 p('Measure RPO as the difference between the last source commit and the latest consistent recovered commit. Measure RTO from the agreed outage start to verified service restoration. Record any data-loss window and merchant reconciliation steps. A server boot time is not the full service recovery time.'),
 note('P: after restoration, verify revoked credentials and deletion/hold registers are applied. Failback is a new controlled change; do not reintroduce an unfenced old database writer.'))

page('11 | Incident response plan','Incident command and activation','A proposed operational plan aligned with the risk-management approach in NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3. [R4]',
 table(['Role','Responsibility'],[['Incident commander','Declares severity, coordinates containment, records decisions and authorizes recovery with service ownership.'],['Technical lead','Scopes systems, preserves evidence, implements containment and validates remediation.'],['Security lead','Assesses compromise, attacker access, credential exposure and monitoring changes.'],['DPO / privacy lead','Determines personal-data impact, coordinates legal analysis and required notifications.'],['Service / finance owner','Assesses transaction, wallet and merchant impact; approves financial reconciliation.'],['Communications lead','Maintains factual internal/customer updates, approval records and next-update time.'],['Evidence custodian','Maintains timeline, hashes, original evidence, access logs and custody transfers.'],['Provider liaison','Raises provider tickets, tracks region/platform incidents and preserves provider evidence.']],[.29,.71]),
 h('Activation triggers'),
 p('Activate for suspected unauthorized access, tenant-data exposure, key compromise, financial-integrity mismatch, ransomware, loss of critical data, sustained service outage or an unsafe external-delivery path. Begin a timestamped incident record even when scope is uncertain. Preserve the distinction between a security incident and a confirmed personal-data breach.'),
 p('Assign actual names, backups, contact routes and authority limits before adoption. Do not put private phone numbers or emergency secrets in a widely distributed manual. Keep the current roster in a restricted operational system accessible during a primary-platform outage.'),
 note('P: these roles and procedures are an operating proposal. Naming them in this manual does not establish a staffed 24/7 response capability.'))

page('11 | Incident response plan','Response workflow','D09 - Preserve evidence, contain impact and recover through explicit validation.',
 diagram('incident','Proposed incident lifecycle. Privacy/legal assessment runs alongside containment and technical investigation; it must not wait for complete root-cause certainty.'),
 p('At triage, record detection time, first known occurrence, affected environment, systems, tenants, data classes and current customer impact. Distinguish confirmed facts from hypotheses. Use a restricted incident channel and document every privileged containment action.'),
 p('Recovery requires evidence that the cause is removed or controlled and that integrity checks pass. Continue heightened monitoring after reopening traffic. Close only when the service owner accepts residual risk, communications are complete and corrective actions have owners and deadlines.'),
 note('P: perform a tabletop exercise before launch and after major architectural changes. Review missed detections, response friction, data reconciliation and communications in each exercise.'))

page('11 | Incident response plan','Severity and first-response playbooks','Proposed response targets require staffing approval and a tested escalation route.',
 table(['Severity','Examples','Initial target / update'],[['SEV1 - critical','Confirmed broad compromise, material financial corruption or core service unavailable','Acknowledge within 15 min; updates every 30 min'],['SEV2 - high','Significant tenant impact, suspected key exposure or growing critical backlog','Acknowledge within 30 min; updates hourly'],['SEV3 - moderate','Limited degradation with a workable alternative and no confirmed data exposure','Acknowledge within 4 business hours; daily updates'],['SEV4 - low','Routine defect, request or documentation issue','Acknowledge within 1 business day; agreed schedule']],[.25,.47,.28]),
 h('IR-01: stolen API credential or account takeover'),
 p('Identify the affected account, key and environment. Revoke the exposed key or session, preserve relevant access and transaction records, and check recent source events, changes to callback endpoints and team grants. Contain the source of exposure before issuing replacement credentials. Reconcile accepted events with the merchant; never erase evidence to hide fraudulent activity.'),
 h('IR-02: malicious callback or SSRF attempt'),
 p('Pause or revoke the endpoint and preserve configuration history, delivery IDs, resolved destinations and sanitized error metadata. Check egress logs for forbidden access and whether DNS pinning or destination validation was bypassed. Verify all endpoints owned by the affected account and retest the fix in isolation before reactivation.'),
 note('P: these acknowledgement times are targets for an approved support model. They are not resolution guarantees or a statement that on-call coverage is already contracted.'))

page('11 | Incident response plan','Financial integrity and outage playbooks','Protect accepted records and prevent a recovery action from creating another incident.',
 h('IR-03: wallet or duplicate-transaction mismatch'),
 p('Freeze the affected write path if continued processing risks further inconsistency. Preserve the intake identity, canonical hash, acceptance result, transaction/receipt IDs, fee policy, wallet entries and audit events. Compare the merchant source outbox to accepted NUERS records. Determine whether the anomaly is a display/query issue, a rejected request, an unknown outcome or an actual ledger discrepancy. Use a reviewed adjustment or corrective release; do not patch posted balances directly.'),
 h('IR-04: ransomware or suspected host compromise'),
 p('Isolate the affected host and credentials without destroying volatile evidence. Stop untrusted replication from overwriting clean recovery material. Identify the earliest trustworthy restore point and independent keys/checkpoints. Rebuild from known artifacts in an isolated environment, validate integrity, rotate exposed credentials and perform privacy review. A clean antivirus result alone is insufficient recovery evidence.'),
 h('IR-05: regional/provider outage or database failure'),
 p('Declare impact, preserve available failure evidence and confirm whether the outage is transient. Fence the old writer before standby promotion. Restore a compatible database/object/key set, reconcile uncertain external side effects and verify balances, sequences and audit chain. Switch traffic only after the authorized recovery decision, then resume workers gradually.'),
 h('IR-06: receipt email sent to the wrong recipient'),
 p('Pause affected mail processing, preserve recipient source and delivery state, identify the exposed document/data classes and prevent further copies. Assess whether a source input, tenant boundary or rendering bug caused the event. Route to the privacy lead and correct the cause before an authorized resend. SMTP acceptance does not prove recall is possible.'),
 note('P: each playbook must name an owner, preserve a timeline, define a rollback/stop condition and capture verification evidence. Do not use production financial writes as a diagnostic shortcut.'))

page('11 | Incident response plan','Breach assessment and communications','Privacy reporting is a qualified legal workflow, not an automatic message for every technical error.',
 p('The Philippine National Privacy Commission describes mandatory notification criteria involving sensitive or identity-fraud-enabling information, reason to believe an unauthorized person acquired it, and a real risk of serious harm. Its guidance provides a 72-hour notification window upon knowledge of or reasonable belief in a qualifying personal-data breach. The DPO/legal lead must assess applicability, current rules, recipients, channel and timing promptly. [R7]'),
 table(['Decision record','Required content - proposed'],[['Detection and scope','What happened, when discovered, affected systems/environment, data categories, known persons/tenants and uncertainty.'],['Notification assessment','Applicable rule, facts supporting or rejecting notification, accountable decision-maker, deadline and next reassessment time.'],['Containment','Actions taken, credentials revoked, services paused and remaining exposure.'],['Recipient communication','Facts, likely impact, protective action, contact route and next update. Avoid speculation or secret/customer data in broad messages.'],['Regulator / contractual notices','Approved notice, submission reference, evidence of timing and follow-up obligations. Track provider and merchant contract requirements separately.'],['Closure','Root cause, affected scope, remediation, lessons and accepted residual risk.']],[.28,.72]),
 p('Proposed initial customer update: "We are investigating an issue affecting [service/environment] since [time and zone]. Known impact: [confirmed facts]. Current action: [containment]. Customer action: [specific instruction or none]. Next update: [time]. Reference: [incident ID]." Obtain the designated approval without delaying legally required notifications.'),
 note('P/V: appoint the privacy lead, maintain current legal guidance and test notification access before an incident. This manual is an operational framework and does not replace case-specific legal assessment.'))

page('12 | Service-level framework','Service catalogue and accountability','Separate the NUERS service from dependencies and outcomes outside its direct control.',
 table(['Service','Responsibility and measurement boundary'],[['Merchant portal','Authenticated access to owned business records and available functions. Measure usable requests and user-visible errors.'],['Transaction intake/status API','Authenticate, validate and durably record an eligible event or return a truthful rejection. Measure the documented API endpoint boundary.'],['Document view / PDF','Render the stored financial record with permitted branding. Measure successful authorized rendering and generation time.'],['Customer receipt email','Queue and attempt an eligible receipt email. Measure queue/SMTP outcomes separately from customer inbox delivery.'],['Merchant webhooks','Deliver signed events to an active verified receiver using bounded retry and visible outcomes. Receiver availability is a separate dependency.'],['Wallet and fee records','Maintain auditable fee and balance movements; expose recorded totals and exceptions.'],['Reports / exports','Produce scoped records and totals consistent with filters and environment.'],['Government transport','Excluded from an operational promise until authoritative interface, eligibility, provider and agreement are established.']],[.30,.70]),
 h('Proposed agreement structure'),
 p('Define service hours, support timezone, covered endpoints, exclusions, maintenance notice, severity model, escalation, measurement source, reporting period, remedies and data exit. The commercial agreement must name the actual accountable entity and support contacts. Specify how disputes about availability and transaction outcomes are resolved using evidence.'),
 p('Provider uptime commitments do not automatically become NUERS application commitments. Likewise, a healthy application does not prove a merchant\'s internet connection, email mailbox, callback receiver or government system is available.'),
 note('P: this chapter is a framework for a future approved SLA and internal SLOs, not a signed agreement or a guarantee of availability.'))

page('12 | Service-level framework','Proposed SLIs, objectives and budgets','Targets become commitments only after measurement, staffing, testing and management approval.',
 table(['Indicator','Definition','Initial proposed objective'],[['Core API availability','Successful eligible intake/status service outcomes / eligible attempts, measured at the service boundary','99.9% monthly'],['Interactive latency','p95 for a defined representative single-event workload under approved load; excludes third-party delivery','2 seconds target; load envelope must be tested'],['Document rendering','p95 for agreed document size/template on the deployed renderer','10 seconds target; benchmark required'],['Dispatch freshness','Time from committed intent to first worker attempt when enabled and endpoint eligible','95% within 2 minutes'],['Financial correctness','Unexplained duplicate debit or accepted-identity conflict','Zero tolerated; every occurrence investigated'],['Recovery point','Latest consistent recovered commit relative to source','At most 15 minutes, subject to drill'],['Recovery time','Agreed outage start to verified service restoration','At most 4 hours, subject to drill']],[.24,.49,.27]),
 p('For a 30-day month, a 99.9% time-based availability objective permits 43 minutes 12 seconds of unavailability. The request-based SLI above has a separate error budget of 0.1% of eligible attempts. Do not interchange request-based and time-based calculations in service reports.'),
 p('Classify expected validation/authentication/business rejections separately from server failures. An invalid payload or insufficient wallet is not automatically a platform outage, but an incorrectly rejected valid request is a service defect. Capture timeouts and unknown outcomes; do not hide them by counting only received 2xx responses.'),
 note('P: agree maintenance treatment and exclusions in writing, keep raw measurements, and review error-budget burn. No performance or availability benchmark was executed to establish these proposed numbers.'))

page('12 | Service-level framework','Support, maintenance and service review','A support process needs clear ownership, a clock and an evidence trail.',
 h('Support intake - proposed'),
 p('Collect incident/request ID, tenant reference, environment, affected operation, time and timezone, sanitized error, correlation/transaction ID and business impact. Do not collect API secrets, MFA codes, full customer documents or unrestricted database exports in an ordinary ticket. Route security/privacy reports to the restricted response team immediately.'),
 table(['Process','Proposed operating rule'],[['Coverage','Approve staffed service hours and an emergency route. Do not advertise 24/7 support until staffing and escalation are contracted and tested.'],['Planned maintenance','Provide at least 48 hours\' notice for material planned interruption where feasible; disclose scope and expected duration. Emergency security changes use the incident route.'],['Escalation','Service desk to technical owner to incident commander/service owner. Unacknowledged SEV1 alerts escalate through a tested secondary route.'],['Change communication','State behavior changes, API compatibility, migration steps, deprecation dates and documentation version.'],['Monthly review','Availability, latency, incidents, queue delivery, backup/restore evidence, wallet reconciliation, access reviews and overdue actions.'],['Exit / portability','Verify authorized export, data format, retained records, key/secret revocation, support closure and provider deletion evidence.']],[.28,.72]),
 p('Keep a status history with incident start/end, affected services and the measurement basis. Report estimated recovery as an estimate, not a guaranteed time. Record separately the point when service returns and the point when all delayed notifications or financial reconciliations finish.'),
 note('P: contractual remedies, service credits, liability, retention commitments and mandatory notices must be finalized by the business and legal owners.'))

page('13 | System operating manual','Merchant administration workflow','A concise end-to-end procedure for business owners and authorized staff.',
 table(['Step','Action and expected result'],[['1. Access','Use a registered email and authorized login. Complete MFA for the login; keep recovery codes private. Add an independent authenticator device through Account Security when needed.'],['2. Business profile','Maintain permitted contact details, website and description. Submit identity/tax changes for administrative review. Update the profile image separately from receipt branding.'],['3. Documents','Upload the required evidence; inspect status and review notes. A replacement is a new reviewable version, not a change to the prior decision.'],['4. Integration','After account eligibility is satisfied, copy assigned connection metadata, create an environment-specific key and store the secret on the ERP/POS backend.'],['5. Sandbox','Submit synthetic cases, test duplicates and errors, and confirm that no Live balance, report or customer email is affected.'],['6. Receipt settings','Upload the dedicated invoice/receipt logo, select permitted layout and save. Preview and download a copy to verify branding.'],['7. Live operation','Keep sufficient wallet funds; submit stable source events and display NUERS success or the returned error reason in the source system.'],['8. Follow-up','Review status, email outcomes, webhook attempts, reports and exports. Escalate uncertain outcomes with record IDs, not secrets.']],[.24,.76]),
 p('The connector should show pending/unknown until it has an authoritative result. Rejected events should display a useful operator-facing reason. A background retry must not conceal a persistent error or create another external reference. Merchant sales reports should use the selected date, environment and sale category consistently.'),
 note('I: workflow capabilities are reflected in the reviewed portal/services. V: screenshots, actual permission assignments and deployment-specific menu wording may differ until the release is verified.'))

page('13 | System operating manual','Administrator and finance workflow','Control identity, evidence and money without confusing account activation with external authority.',
 bullets('Review the registered merchant and uploaded document versions. Open the evidence, record an independent decision and use a factual reason. Keep the reviewer and timestamp.','Approve account access and onboarding only through the authorized workflow. An already active/onboarded account should not be presented as requiring a duplicate account approval merely because a generic document banner is stale.','Process proposed identity/profile corrections with the appropriate evidence. Synchronize the approved owner/representative display through the supported workflow; do not directly alter unrelated user records.','Inspect connected merchant source profiles, API environments and status. Default owned-system metadata does not authorize an unrelated historical source or government issuance.','Configure the transaction minimum and fee with a reason. A minimum of zero charges every new eligible Live primary sale at the configured fee; reconcile actual charged amounts from the ledger.','Review deposits and wallet adjustments with the required approvals and evidence. Separate available balance, deposited funds and recorded fee revenue in reports.','Use filters and exports consistently: business, date range/timezone, environment, document type, status and applicable sales-benefit category. Verify pagination stays below the table and does not alter the exported scope.'),
 h('Report and export governance'),
 p('Senior citizen, PWD, national athletes/coaches, solo parent and manual-discount data must preserve their recorded bases and financial amounts. A category label alone is not a legal eligibility decision. Use the current calculation and integration contract, document source evidence and avoid combining supplementary payment receipts with primary invoices as new sales.'),
 note('P: periodically compare dashboard totals, detailed exports and ledger queries for the same filter context. Maintain export access logs and an approved recipient policy.'))

page('14 | Validation and readiness','Test strategy and acceptance evidence','A detailed manual must identify how its claims will be verified.',
 table(['Test family','Minimum scenarios'],[['Authorization','Cross-tenant reads/writes, role downgrade, team limits, object-ID guessing, exports, revoked sessions and keys.'],['Identity / MFA','Unknown Google email, mismatched subject, single-use handoff, replayed TOTP, expired setup, five-device limit and recovery-code reuse.'],['Financial correctness','Zero/positive fee threshold, exact boundary, insufficient funds, concurrent requests, rounding, manual discounts, mixed tax and sales-benefit cases.'],['Durable intake','Same identity/same content, changed content conflict, timeout after commit, crash before commit, batch rollback and unchanged retry.'],['Environment','Live/Sandbox status visibility, reports, wallet, numbering, email suppression and callback separation.'],['Documents','Large upload, corrupt frame/hash, unauthorized preview, replacement history, independent logo and shared PDF rendering.'],['Delivery','Valid/invalid signature, replay, private DNS, redirect, timeout, rotation, dead letters, mail uncertainty and worker restart.'],['Recovery','Restore database/objects/keys, audit checkpoint, lost-write window, stale credentials, deletion register and paused side effects.']],[.26,.74]),
 p('Record release digest, environment, test data classification, commands, expected/actual outcomes, timestamps and evidence locations. Use isolated synthetic fixtures for destructive and failure-injection tests. A repository test file proves only that a test exists; a dated successful run and its scope are required for a release claim.'),
 note('V: no new full application test suite, penetration test, capacity benchmark or live recovery drill was executed solely to produce this manual. PDF layout/content validation is a separate document-quality activity.'))

page('14 | Validation and readiness','Deployment readiness and risks','Resolve these items before representing the planned architecture as production-assured.',
 table(['Priority / item','Required closure evidence','Accountable role'],[['Critical: actual hosting/residency','Signed provider/region register for app, database, objects, backups, logs, keys and support','Service owner + DPO'],['Critical: recovery and key custody','Independent restoration, measured RPO/RTO, key recovery and fenced failover test','Platform + security'],['Critical: official transport claims','Authoritative provider specification, credentials, eligibility and authentic acknowledgement tests','Compliance + integration'],['High: operational staffing','Named on-call roster, escalation drill, incident contacts and approved SLA scope','Service owner'],['High: external customer delivery','Mail setup/domain evidence and real authorized receiver verification; privacy/data-location record','Operations + merchant'],['High: runtime hardening','TLS/firewall review, origin restriction, DB grants, storage encryption and secret handling','Platform + security'],['High: independent assurance','Negative access tests, vulnerability review, dependency inventory and remediation records','Security lead'],['High: capacity and monitoring','Agreed load envelope, measured performance, alerts, queue limits and restore capacity','Engineering + operations'],['Medium: governance','Retention schedule, access reviews, control approvals and documentation/version ownership','Records owner + DPO']],[.34,.43,.23]),
 p('Do not turn these items into green status simply by approving the document. Each requires evidence from the actual deployment, responsible operator or external authority. Record compensating controls and expiry for any accepted exception, and revisit the risk after material changes.'),
 note('I/P/V separation is intentional: application capability, a proposed policy and deployed assurance are different evidence levels.'))

page('14 | Validation and readiness','Operating approvals and ownership','Use this page to adopt the proposed controls only after the identified evidence is available.',
 table(['Approval area','Named owner / approval reference','Status in this edition'],[['Software release baseline','Engineering owner and immutable release digest','To be recorded'],['Hosting and data location','Service owner and signed deployment register','Planned; allocation unconfirmed'],['Security / key custody','Security owner and control evidence pack','To be reviewed'],['Privacy and retention','DPO/legal owner and processing/retention register','To be reviewed'],['Incident response','Incident commander roster and completed exercise','Proposed'],['Business continuity','Recovery owner and independent restore evidence','Proposed targets; unmeasured'],['Service levels','Business owner and executed service agreement','Framework only'],['Government interface','Authorized compliance/integration owner and provider evidence','Default software transport blocked'],['Document release','Document controller, review date and distribution list','Prepared for review']],[.29,.44,.27]),
 p('A signature or approval reference should identify the scope accepted, unresolved exceptions, evidence location and next review date. Do not sign on behalf of an unassigned role. A reviewer can approve the manual as an accurate baseline while leaving the proposed hosting or SLA decisions pending.'),
 h('Change triggers'),
 p('Review this manual after an API/schema change, new provider or region, data-store migration, encryption/key change, authentication change, material incident, recovery exercise or revised legal/contractual requirement. Keep the website guidance, merchant connection PDF, OpenAPI schema and deployed behavior aligned with a common release record.'),
 note('P: suggested review cycle is quarterly and after material change. Retain superseded editions with their evidence references; remove outdated public copies when replacements are published.'))

